# Every text file checks out with LF, on every platform.
#
# This is not a style preference - it is what makes the byte-stability ratchet mean
# anything. A copybook resolved from the local search path is read as BYTES and its length
# goes into both retrieval reports (`bytes: 513`). Left to git's autocrlf, a Windows
# checkout gets CRLF and that count is one byte per line larger than the same checkout on
# Linux - so `tools/gate.py` fails on a clone that changed nothing, and the reports this
# tool emits differ by where it was cloned. The parse bundle this repo consumes through
# `--from-parse` embeds the source verbatim and pins it with a sha256, so the same applies
# there (mainframe-common pins it for the same reason).
#
# COBOL is fixed-format source besides, where a byte count is a fact about the member
# rather than an artifact of the filesystem it landed on.
#
# The sibling front-ends (eztrieve, cics, asm) have pinned this from the start; this repo
# had not, so its report goldens were only ever valid on a CRLF checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the files whose exact bytes are the contract.
*.py      text eol=lf
*.cbl     text eol=lf
*.cpy     text eol=lf
*.mjs     text eol=lf
*.js      text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.toml    text eol=lf
*.sha256  text eol=lf
*.txt     text eol=lf
